speechtext:
1.	What control, if any, did the national gallery of Canada exercise over the selection of exhibits in a collection of paintings, loaned for display by Walter Chrysler, Jr., entitled The Controversial Century 1850-1950?
2.	Did any official of the national gallery receive a warning or admonition respecting the authenticity of any of the works exhibited and, if so, who gave the warning, who received it, and when?
3.	If the answer to part 2 is yes, what, if any, investigation into authenticity was made by the national gallery before lending the gallery's prestige to that display?
4.	What, if any, investigation into authenticity was made or is being made by the national gallery of the works, galleries, dealers and experts, cited in the official catalogue, respecting disputed works, before lending the gallery's prestige to the publication of the catalogue and, in particular:
(a)	respecting the paintings attributed to Georges Seurat, "Croqueton pour 'La Grande Jatte' ", "Seated Figure of a Man and Woman", "Seated Man on Bank of River", (i) was any investigation made of the record of the alleged sale by the "Hotel Drouot" (ii) are the paintings listed in the Dorra-Rewald catalogue of Seurat's works published in 1961;
(b)	respecting the paintings attributed to Alfred Sisley, "Maisons a Veneux-Nadon, en etc" and "Winter Landscape" (i) was any investigation made of the alleged collection of "Laurent, Nantes, France" and of "Trotti and Co., Paris" (ii) are these paintings listed in Daulte's definitive catalogue of Sisley works published in 1959;
(c)	respecting the painting attributed to Hilaire Germain Edgar Degas, "Sketch of a Young Girl", was any investigation made of the alleged collection of "Lucien LeFebure-Foiret, Paris";
(d)	respecting a painting attributed to Andre Derain, "Landscape with Tree", was any investigation made of the alleged collection of "A. Bien, Paris";
(e)	respecting the painting attributed to Jean Desire Gustave Courbet, "Still Life with Pottery", was any investigation made of the alleged collection of "Coulette, Paris";
(f)	respecting the paintings attributed to Claude Oscar Monet, "Une Partie du Portail de la Cathe-drale de Rouen" and "Notre Dame de Neige", was any investigation made (i) that they in fact came from the collection, as alleged, of "Etienne Moreau-Neloton, Paris" (ii) of the alleged collection of "William Guillaume, Paris, 1949" (iii) of the alleged experts "J. Subert" and "Maurice Marignane, Paris";
(g)	respecting the paintings attributed to Berthe Morisot, "Jeune Fille avec Chapeau" and "Jeune Fille dans le Jardin", do the paintings appear in the Bataille-Wildenstein catalogue of the works of Morisot;
(h)	respecting the paintings attributed to Pierre Auguste Renoir, "Still Life" and "On Leave", was any investigation made of the alleged collection of "Leonard Cauparde, Paris, 1961" and "William Guillaume, Paris, 1949";
(i)	respecting the painting attributed to Pablo Ruiz y Picasso, "Cubist Composition", was any investigation made of the alleged collection of "M. Bien, Paris, 1958";
(j)	was any investigation made respecting one "Louis Aertz", or of a gallery of that name, alleged to be located in Paris, respecting the authentication of "The Yellow Christ" attributed to Maurice Denis, "Christ and Disciples on the Road to Emmaus" attributed to Georges Rouault, "Vase of Flowers" attributed to Jean Edouard Vuillard and "Winter Landscape" attributed to Alfred Sisley;

(k)	respecting the painting attributed to Honors Daumier, "Character Study", was any investigation made that it did in fact come from a collection, as alleged, of "Peter Hawkins, Paris, 1962";
(l)	respecting the painting attributed to Roger Noel Francois de La Fresnaye, "White Houses on Hill", was any investigation made that it did in fact come from a collection, as alleged, of "Low-man, Paris, 1949";
(m)	respecting the painting attributed to Camille Jacob Pissarro, "Le Village", was any investigation made that it did in fact come from a collection as alleged of "Mr. Mayer, Paris, 1949"?
5.	When returned to the United States, what obligation, if any, will the gallery incur for payment of duty or replacement value of the disputed paintings?
6.	What was the cost to the national gallery of Canada of shipping, insuring, and displaying these disputed works?
